Use appropriate TypeScript types and error handling. Make database operations reusable instead of scattering direct database calls across UI components.

## 4. DATABASE DESIGN

Design a sensible relational schema for a modest-fashion e-commerce business.

Inspect existing tables before proposing migrations. Reuse compatible existing tables where appropriate. Avoid creating duplicate tables for the same purpose.

At minimum, support the following entities.

### A. Products

Store:
- Stable product ID.
- Product name.
- Unique slug.
- Short description.
- Full description.
- Category reference.
- Main image.
- Additional product images.
- Base price.
- Optional sale price.
- Currency code.
- Publication status.
- Featured status.
- Optional product SKU.
- Search-friendly metadata where appropriate.
- Created timestamp.
- Updated timestamp.

Use suitable database constraints and indexes, including a unique slug.

Support unpublished products that remain invisible to ordinary storefront visitors.

### B. Categories

Store:
- Category ID.
- Category name.
- Unique slug.
- Description.
- Optional category image.
- Display order.
- Active/inactive status.
- Created and updated timestamps.

Potential initial categories, subject to actual inventory:
- Abayas.
- Khimars.
- Veils.
- Hijabs.
- Caps.
- Accessories.

Do not create misleading public categories containing no published products unless the interface intentionally supports empty categories.

### C. Product variations

Support products that have different sizes and colours.

A variation may contain:
- Variation ID.
- Product ID.
- SKU where available.
- Size, when applicable.
- Colour, when applicable.
- Optional variation-specific price.
- Optional sale price.
- Stock quantity.
- Active/inactive status.
- Created and updated timestamps.

Use the variation structure to distinguish stock for different sizes and colours.

For example, a black abaya in size M and the same abaya in size L must be capable of having different stock quantities.

Do not require sizes for products that do not use sizes. Support products with colour-only, size-only, or no variations.

Prevent duplicate variation combinations for the same product where applicable.

Use appropriate constraints to prevent invalid negative stock quantities.

### D. Product images

Support an ordered list of product images, or a normalized product-image table if that fits the existing schema better.

Support:
- Main image selection.
- Additional gallery images.
- Image display order.
- Alt text.
- Removing and replacing images.

Avoid duplicating the same image URL unnecessarily.

### E. Store settings

Create a suitable settings structure for configurable store information, including:
- Store display name.
- Brand tagline.
- Logo URL.
- Homepage announcement.
- Contact email, if supplied.
- Business phone or WhatsApp destination, when verified.
- WhatsApp catalogue URL.
- Instagram URL.
- Business address.
- Default currency.
- Storefront display preferences.

Use the business information already supplied in the project where it is available. Do not invent missing contact information.

Treat sensitive configuration carefully. Public storefront settings must not expose private credentials.

### F. Homepage content

Support editable homepage content where appropriate:
- Hero heading.
- Hero supporting text.
- Hero image.
- Primary CTA label and destination.
- Secondary CTA label and destination.
- Announcement text.
- Featured product selection.
- Featured category selection.
- Editorial section heading and copy.
- Publication status.

Ensure editable content cannot accidentally introduce unsafe HTML or arbitrary script execution.

### G. Optional future entities

Do not overbuild the database. Do not add unnecessary tables for payment processing, customer accounts, reviews, shipping integrations, or order management unless the current project already has an established requirement for them.

The immediate business model uses WhatsApp to discuss and confirm customer orders. Do not add an online payment gateway.

